<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutsamudu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutsamudu is the capital of the island of Anjouan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> and the country's second-largest town. Founded in the 15th century, it became the seat of the Anjouan sultans, with a stone medina, palaces and a citadel built in the late 18th century above its harbour. It was a regular port of call for ships on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutsamudu.jpg" alt="Mutsamudu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peioma · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mutsamudu_port1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mutsamudu, also written Moutsamoudou, was likely founded in 1482. It grew into a prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> port that funnelled traffic between the Swahili ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. Later, Arab, Persian, Indian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and Malagasy sailors and traders settled in the town. By the 1700s it was the most prosperous town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> archipelago.</p><p>In 1773 the commissioner Frederick Holtzappel visited it during an expedition along the Swahili Coast and recorded his admiration. He noted that the houses were built of stone, whitewashed inside and out, with painted plank ceilings and roofs thatched with coconut leaves. He saw several two-storey houses belonging to the elite, whom he called Turkified. There were numerous public baths in the town, and every elite household had its own. The men wore white linen gowns and turbans, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> clothing and daggers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> sheaths. The citadel was built in 1786 with British help to protect the town from Malagasy slave raisers, and it was badly damaged in 1950 at the landfall of a cyclone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mutsamudu has two parallel main streets, with the medina of the 15th century lying between them, and narrow streets with many shops and small crafts. It is the capital and largest city of the island of Anjouan and the second-largest city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>. Its deep-water port, built in 1982, is the only one in the Union of the Comoros; three quarters of its cargo is containerised goods transhipped to the other two islands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a>, cement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, flour and petroleum products come in through it, and ylang-ylang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and vanilla go out. The climate is marine tropical, with temperatures from 27°C to 32°C throughout the year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mutsamudu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling house later moved to <b>Mutsamudu</b> on the north-west coast, which grew into a fortified stone town with a citadel overlooking its harbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-anjouan">Sultanate of Anjouan</a></li></ul></section>
